flexbox-sketch-0.bodyMarkdown:
A row flex container normally tries to keep all items on one line. `flex-wrap: wrap` lets items move to another flex line when the container cannot fit them comfortably.

```css
nav {
  flex-wrap: wrap;
}
```

**Line by line:** the navigation remains a row flex container. The wrap declaration permits additional lines rather than forcing every link into one crowded line.

flexbox-sketch-1.bodyMarkdown:
The portfolio already has semantic sections and articles styled as cards. The parent can become a wrapping flex container without replacing those elements.

```css
main {
  flex-direction: row;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 1.5rem;
}
```

**Line by line:** changing the main axis to row lets cards sit beside one another. Wrapping allows later cards to move to another line, and gap creates consistent spacing between them.

flexbox-sketch-2.bodyMarkdown:
`flex` can describe how a flex item grows, shrinks, and what base size it starts from. A flexible basis is useful for cards that should share a row but remain readable.

```css
main > section,
main > article {
  flex: 1 1 18rem;
}
```

**Line by line:** the grouped selector targets existing semantic cards. The first `1` allows growth, the second allows shrinking, and `18rem` supplies the starting flex basis.
